            {# ── Slide 3: Voice-first ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-4" data-slide-id="3" data-video-start="45" data-video-end="65">
                <p class="pi-label">The gesture that started this</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">It started with a voice note<br>on a walk.</h2>
                <p class="pi-lede">Voice is how Parachute meets you. Substrate is what Parachute is.</p>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>The simplest interface: <strong>press a button, talk</strong>. No app to open, no cursor, no prompt window. The thought fades fast; the gesture has to be faster.</p>
                    <p>That's where Parachute started, and it's still the front door for most people. A voice-first journal &mdash; capture as you walk, capture as you wake up, capture before the meeting starts. Local transcription by default. Audio never leaves your device unless you ask it to.</p>
                    <p>But voice was the gesture, not the product. The product is what happens to the words after they're transcribed &mdash; where they live, who can read them, what compounds over time.</p>
                </aside>
            </section>

            {# ── Slide 4: What's broken ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-5" data-slide-id="4" data-video-start="65" data-video-end="95">
                <p class="pi-label">Why this is missing today</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">Every layer above the data<br>is fighting for the data.</h2>
                <div class="pi-traps">
                    <div class="pi-trap">
                        <strong>Notion traps you</strong>
                        <span>Your knowledge in their database. Their AI on top. Their export rules. Switching costs grow with every doc.</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-trap">
                        <strong>Obsidian loses you</strong>
                        <span>Markdown files in folders, no AI bridge. Every tool starts cold. The power users love it; everyone else gives up.</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-trap">
                        <strong>ChatGPT forgets you</strong>
                        <span>"Memory" is a flat text file with your name and a few preferences. Every new chat starts over. You re-explain yourself for the rest of your life.</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-trap">
                        <strong>Vendor agents lock you in</strong>
                        <span>Operator runs in their cloud. Tasks live in their projects. Cowork data goes to their server. The agents are getting more powerful and your leverage is shrinking.</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>The tools you're using already are not on your side, and the friction shows up in the same shapes &mdash; trap, lose, forget, lock in.</p>
                    <p>Parachute is the substrate underneath. Same data, different relationship: <strong>yours, on your machine, accessible to every agent and interface you choose to point at it.</strong></p>
                </aside>
            </section>

            {# ── Slide 5: The Parachute substrate ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-5" data-slide-id="5" data-video-start="95" data-video-end="130">
                <p class="pi-label">The substrate</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">Spin up a vault.<br>Give any AI a link.</h2>
                <div class="pi-img">
                    {# Reused from old NVC deck slide 5: phone/browser/pendant → Vault → Claude/ChatGPT/Gemini.
                       TODO: refresh diagram for current ecosystem — Hub-as-issuer + the four modules + scoped JWTs. #}
                    <picture>
                        <source type="image/webp"
                                srcset="/assets/pitch/optimized/vault-architecture-800w.webp 800w,
                                        /assets/pitch/optimized/vault-architecture-1600w.webp 1600w,
                                        /assets/pitch/optimized/vault-architecture-2400w.webp 2400w"
                                sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60vw">
                        <img src="/assets/pitch/optimized/vault-architecture-1600w.webp"
                             alt="Vault architecture: phone, browser, pendant connect to Parachute Vault, which connects via MCP to Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini"
                             loading="lazy">
                    </picture>
                    <p class="pi-img-caption">Architecture diagram &mdash; any client over MCP. Refresh planned to show Hub-as-issuer + the four modules + scoped JWTs.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="pi-flywheel">
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Vault</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>The graph.</strong> Notes, tags, links. Search and traversal.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Scribe</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>The ear.</strong> Whisper-compatible transcription. Local by default.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Notes</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>The reference interface.</strong> PWA, offline, voice capture.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Hub</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body"><strong>The front door.</strong> Install, start, stop, expose. OAuth issuer with scope-narrowed JWTs.</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>A <strong>vault</strong> is a knowledge graph that runs on your machine. SQLite-backed. Wikilinks, full-text search, semantic search. Reachable over <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MCP</a> &mdash; the open standard every major lab now supports &mdash; and over a plain REST API. Any AI can read and write to it.</p>
                    <p>Around the vault, four small modules form the rest of the substrate. Notes is a reference interface, not a requirement &mdash; build your own.</p>
                    <p>And underneath it all, a small <strong>module protocol</strong> &mdash; <code>info</code>, <code>config</code>, <code>services.json</code>, <code>well-known</code> &mdash; that any third-party module can implement and plug in. Calendar, email, your own knowledge bases, your own agents. The hub renders them like they were native.</p>
                    <p>Hub-as-issuer is the part that's quietly load-bearing: <strong>one identity, many narrowly-scoped tokens</strong>. A Notes app gets read+write to the vault. A read-only AI client gets read. A third-party module gets exactly the scope it asked for. You stop trusting tools transitively.</p>
                </aside>
            </section>

            {# ── Slide 6: The flywheel ── #}
            <section class="pi-slide fade-up fade-up-5" data-slide-id="6" data-video-start="130" data-video-end="155">
                <p class="pi-label">How it compounds</p>
                <h2 class="pi-h2">Vault at the center.<br>Agents and interfaces orbit.</h2>
                <div class="pi-flywheel">
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Capture</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body">Voice (Scribe), type (Notes), drop a file, sync from elsewhere. The graph grows with how you actually think.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Read</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body">Claude Code, claude.ai, ChatGPT, Codex, Goose, Cursor, Zed &mdash; anyone speaking MCP. Custom agents and custom UIs you build yourself.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Compose</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body">Third-party modules plug in. Calendar, email, knowledge bases, niche tools. The hub wires them, scopes them, renders their config.</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pi-flywheel-row">
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-role">Compound</div>
                        <div class="pi-flywheel-body">The graph gets richer. Every read makes the next read better. The substrate is the part that doesn't get cloned next quarter.</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <aside class="pi-notes">
                    <p>The vault is the substrate. Everything else is an orbit around it. Capture grows the graph; reads compound on it; third-party modules compose new behavior on top; and over time the graph itself is the moat &mdash; the part that doesn't get cloned next quarter.</p>
                </aside>
